#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189166} :activity/id #uuid "6c929710-b1e7-526f-9174-4bfbb38ad67d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049623 :topic/id #uuid "063ad68a-8d74-5b0a-bcd4-0761bdbda990" :topic/math-academy-id 3896} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188024 :question/id #uuid "d97c3cf2-b627-5274-853a-be3d7e5cda40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10246"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570710}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"} :step/id #uuid "a90d4984-452a-525d-98ff-d143511f4019" :step/math-academy-id 38627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188025 :question/id #uuid "b28b6939-847d-51ab-a97d-4367323d0d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-10247"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570778}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"} :step/id #uuid "7561e93c-2e69-5be7-8738-113baba7f21c" :step/math-academy-id 38630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188026 :question/id #uuid "71cc1f65-437f-5928-82c2-be3dc4b0985f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10248"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562483}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"} :step/id #uuid "2a1a74dc-1686-5d5f-ac6a-f9e7728dad03" :step/math-academy-id 38631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188027 :question/id #uuid "708db737-4215-5df0-8940-c9d17dbd2b5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10249"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562490}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"} :step/id #uuid "5e0aa8b0-5bd9-5bc7-b889-cd7fc9160f2d" :step/math-academy-id 38632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189392 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, when finding the global extrema of a function defined on a closed, bounded interval $[a,b]$ from the graph of its derivative, we need to integrate from right to left instead of left to right when applying the fundamental theorem of calculus. This occurs when we're given the value of $f$ at some value other than the left endpoint.\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ that's defined and differentiable on $[-4,4].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/eb31641d86fbe970ef843372d950c38b425a29c236f8e7aed6895fd57bf7959a.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(4) = 8.$ Notice that we're given that value of $f(x)$ at the *right* endpoint.\n\nLet's use the candidates test to find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4].$\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the right endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(4)\t= 8\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(1),$ we apply the alternate form of the fundamental theorem of calculus that we saw earlier, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to compute $f(-4),$ we apply the alternate form of the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8-(-6+4) \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =8+2 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{8, 4, 10\\} = 10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{max} = 10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max} = -4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f06db8c8-0b3f-5373-ba28-198089dec800" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6388 :tutorial/title "Optimizing a Function Using the Graph of its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "03774e23-8df0-5c17-8990-0fcab5522e04" :step/math-academy-id 38644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189400 :tutorial/content "Recall that if a function $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is continuous over $[a,b],$ the fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + \\int_{a}^{x} f'(t) \\: \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in [a,b].$ This form of the fundamental theorem of calculus is useful if we know $f'(x)$ and the value of $f(a).$ \n\nNow, suppose we're given $f(b)$ instead of $f(a).$ To rewrite the fundamental theorem in this case, we replace $a$ with $b$ in the above, and we get\n\n$$\nf(x)  = f(b) + \\int_{b}^{x} f'(t) \\: \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThis form of the fundamental theorem of calculus will be helpful in this lesson, as we'll see." :tutorial/id #uuid "d98d09c0-ed4c-569c-a8a0-ecd73d2438d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6429 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "60ff00af-e5d1-5663-af4b-9b4002987fb8" :step/math-academy-id 38834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189152}}] :activity/title "Further Optimizing Functions Using Graphs of Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188486}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562577} :activity/id #uuid "5870eef6-66a6-5267-a4c5-8d6d376b114f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049623 :topic/id #uuid "063ad68a-8d74-5b0a-bcd4-0761bdbda990" :topic/math-academy-id 3896} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f13395-bb5b-5cb7-9ee3-5858a0482aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562031} :answer-field/id #uuid "64590d23-2a3d-57b0-ad84-82063765d606" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c6e54a6-aeaf-5247-9068-bf68741c13b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344177" :question/problem "![](images/75/7571f7cb43a21a8369fc9b8197c25788a4005242d373aba2ffce44e7f6221cd6.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $8$ and $3,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $g(2)=4,$ what is the minimum value ${g}_{\\text{min}}$ of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]?$\n\n${g}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $g(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**:  Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & =4 \\\\ g(-1) & =g(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-3) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(-6) & =g(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(8+(-3)) \\\\  & =4-5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,7,-1\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{min}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-6.$"} :step/id #uuid "34f4e575-17c0-53d6-bf0b-41cef82d3ff6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e9434cb-bb9c-5324-9bf4-b108c900c3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561973} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a41a06a-2eac-576e-969d-bc422a8f4058" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bfdcc57a-5fbc-57f0-b88e-30c636d05052" :question/math-academy-id "q-344468" :question/problem "![](images/36/36a84f32380e5735b130f6b8fce26655738a0c86b210a732893dc8500d9cbbf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/cf/cf8e3aa5a6000966c44b36071b2269540578cf123a19024a20a62fb61896dc51.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3-(-16) \\\\  & =13 \\\\ f(5) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,13,1\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "b34474a6-a092-595e-81ba-f0cf9597bd95"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079ed11e-518b-573c-aa01-ad6a596181cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561606} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb43d11-d981-5b1d-a441-dc7bb7487b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561658} {:answer/id #uuid "b64dbb57-aa2e-57ce-8ba1-baf93f0d0a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562036} {:answer/id #uuid "be43a691-2e56-56b2-9c4c-68d6f001fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562068} {:answer/id #uuid "c0474143-18b2-5d56-a3ca-e652dce65abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562069} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b92d65-4457-5e1c-8c99-2be1b9018bda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b70ed03e-6463-5d38-ae5e-1f3dea3b3eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104053" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8131b2ca68e378ef96c1b57e655db0a89d6e5207f0ac842612c64b30a963912.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1,$ $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(1)=-12.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the candidates are  $x=-2,-1,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-12 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2) \\\\  & =-12+2 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2+1) \\\\  & =-12+1 \\\\  & =-11 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12-5 \\\\  & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-12,-10,-11,-17\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is  ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "d4e316e6-be69-5a8b-9bfa-15c379ef577c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae9fb4d-e189-5423-8d13-3a85f24bfe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562089} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d9913c-f4ac-5073-baaf-6eb787330138" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "69626226-2183-5bc4-b118-9ee330e48ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344169" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a971f2e8581ad9a623a8c8541ee1fcae5911fa0d3ae1b91923d19612d74ec9c.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-7,-1].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $14.$\n\nGiven that $s(-1)=3,$ what is the minimum value ${s}_{\\text{min}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[-7,-1]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $s(x)$ on $[-7,-1]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-7,-1),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-7,-1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(-1) & =3 \\\\ s(-7) & =s(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-7}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =s(-1)-{\\int }_{-7}^{-1}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(-14) \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{17,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "e9bbfa59-99fe-599d-bb21-d9652af49793" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562528}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68ad5e9-7fe7-596a-8c2b-19ee3b81de60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185785} {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} {:answer/id #uuid "23daea13-16e4-5115-a876-009e017926b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185787} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24e81a-7cd0-52ae-a4ec-7a2d4936cd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185788} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecf83c7-e917-54b8-9b79-79ccd5e0603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb7bcb5-fc17-5e6e-81c2-7dacca31243a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c577835a-f79d-5fd0-ba9c-ce150ab9e53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144828" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function comprises $2$ triangles, $2$ rectangles, and $1$ trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/99/99f558b3c1d86c5638543361a63823f13a9e01f2cc5042d0f781080f9f33c8a2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+2+4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}\\}=\\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{19}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ee64bc37-0183-5fdf-9406-c969e1bd6184" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562585}}] :activity/title "Further Optimizing Functions Using Graphs of Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561566}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570796} :activity/id #uuid "07f38657-8d82-53c8-a490-bb5a42290b31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049623 :topic/id #uuid "063ad68a-8d74-5b0a-bcd4-0761bdbda990" :topic/math-academy-id 3896} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf2092b-4dd8-5577-b36a-cd5c90777daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570225} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc84bcf-3d54-5493-bbf5-f15198a48f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570263} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccbd482-6f82-5365-be1b-29c7904b3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570313} {:answer/id #uuid "ba49c28a-0d1a-51dd-9f2d-4aaa2b5101b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570453} {:answer/id #uuid "ff533885-694c-5d8f-b455-8987426b0493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570313} :answer-field/id #uuid "920aa12b-f974-5b8f-8512-f6303ba5b5ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25bfd26a-4014-54ec-bb25-41bebf6a0072" :question/math-academy-id "q-144847" :question/problem "![](images/86/861e191e0f0b0d993e2d16f29da17ec9e258d657f6bdfc471e5fa9cf21a67b21.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/b7/b7f6e2f43ae474ec3194b2395144e8ea7dc9964da65bd018af08bb24b873bdd4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-1+5) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,-1,0\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "0a11be39-e15b-5695-a209-6aa5aa9d56b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570865}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ed659-a234-5d78-b3eb-254df1986447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570385} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fef5802-c8af-5407-b40d-c7abf62f6135" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df0b416e-f70a-5924-a6c6-f62bc6887f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344165" :question/problem "![](images/73/731237ee8d4ea5fdb53c50971bad11dd9f4317f595ae9af95846b17b6d064fce.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$\n\nGiven that $g(3)=4,$ what is the minimum value ${g}_{\\text{min}}$ of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${g}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & =4 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-7 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$"} :step/id #uuid "6c8a0b71-476e-5aff-ab08-21790a69690d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23045462-0c63-5e55-a547-f6d9c1423dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570188} {:answer/id #uuid "4271de1f-2b94-5b54-9703-cefae48d132f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570237} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a2d1e-d9c5-580c-ac02-4274a8f8aea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570319} {:answer/id #uuid "97cf76f8-44e3-5bf4-98db-081710e81c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570392} {:answer/id #uuid "f3763ea8-131b-51ba-8034-87fce394cdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aff9ed3-8040-515b-8916-8efa69b012c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5fcb87aa-38a0-5213-a001-cd7978756f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-144875" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cba90791c239c986390a8a7fe1cb3ea0b4abb9cfd86ca13cba9ece6a5b4860b6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ given that $f(4)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a8/a8bff8783c0e536cd0e3c475a8fca87a9861a1e1f0ca7f2833ca9c434da2426d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(-8) \\\\  & =1+8 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(9+2-8) \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,9,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$"} :step/id #uuid "dcee01d3-9728-5373-bd94-47606309bf0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570831}}] :activity/title "Further Optimizing Functions Using Graphs of Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570101}]}
